{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>That he be not as Korah and his assembly, etc.</b> Rashi explained: that he not be stricken with tzara'at as Moses was stricken with tzara'at in his hand (Sanhedrin 110a). And the word 'to him' refers back to the one who disputes the priesthood. And here the student asks: what relevance does the tzara'at of Moses have to one who disputes the priesthood? It would have been more fitting had he brought as proof the tzara'at of Miriam, which was of the very kind of Korah's dispute — for his dispute about the priesthood follows from his disputing the prophecy of Moses, [namely Moses' claim,] 'YHWH did not send you' — and from precisely this species was the tzara'at of Miriam, for she said: 'Has YHWH indeed spoken only with Moses? Has He not spoken also with us?' (Numbers 12:2), and likewise said Korah: 'For all the congregation, every one of them, is holy' (Numbers 16:3) — it is not you alone who heard at Mount Sinai 'I am' and 'You shall have no other gods.' But the tzara'at of Moses' hand — what connection does it have to the dispute of Korah?" "And now lift up your eyes and see, for behold I bring the matters near and they shall be as one in your hand: the like shall be stricken through the like, and from there you shall take sound and good proof that one who disputes the prophecy of Moses is stricken with tzara'at. This is because our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shabbat 97a): one who suspects the worthy is stricken in his body — whence do we know it? From Moses, etc. And according to the line of justice, it is fitting to do to the suspecter as he plotted to do to his fellow. If so, the suspecter is stricken with the very blow that would have been fitting to come upon the suspected party had the matter been true. Now Moses suspected Israel of not believing in his prophecy, as it is written (Exodus 4:1): 'But they will not believe me, etc., for they will say: YHWH has not appeared to you' — and that is precisely the language of Korah, who said: YHWH has not appeared to you, and you are fabricating everything from your own heart. Hence Moses needed to say, 'That YHWH has sent me, for it is not from my own heart' — thus Moses suspected Israel of denying his prophecy and of saying: YHWH has not appeared to you, and you are fabricating from your own heart in order to exercise dominion over us. And from this we see that Moses was stricken with tzara'at for suspecting them of what was not in them — hear from this that had the matter been true, that Israel would have denied his prophecy, then Israel would have been stricken with this same tzara'at; and because he suspected them of what was not in them, he was stricken with the very blow that would have been fitting to come upon them — for if not, it would not be measure for measure, and it must be 'as he plotted to do to his fellow.' And from here is a clear proof that one who disputes the priesthood is stricken with tzara'at, for every one who disputes the priesthood disputes the prophecy of Moses and says: YHWH did not appear to him to give him authority to grant the priesthood to Aaron specifically, and he suspects that Moses gave it to his brother on his own. And this is a precious interpretation." "And the reason for tzara'at specifically is as follows: for we find (Arakhin 16a) that tzara'at comes upon arrogance and upon evil tongue, as indeed Uzziah was stricken because his heart was lifted up to destruction (II Chronicles 26:19), and from the words of Kabbalah we derive, as it is written: 'An arrogant man shall not dwell' (Habakkuk 2:5) — he shall not remain in his dwelling, because his end is to sit alone outside the camp. And if Israel had not believed in the prophecy of Moses, presumably they would have suspected him that in order to seize power over them he wished to draw them after him. And had they said to him, 'YHWH has not appeared to you,' directly to his face, they would surely have been included among the speakers of evil tongue. And from these two sides they would have been stricken with tzara'at, and therefore the judgment turned back upon Moses for suspecting them unjustly. And tzara'at in the hand specifically, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Arakhin 15b): it is written, 'Death and life are in the hand of the tongue' (Proverbs 18:21) — now is there a hand belonging to the tongue? Rather, just as the hand kills, so too the tongue kills, as it is written (Jeremiah 9:7): 'Their tongue is a sharpened arrow' — 'sharpened' is written שׁוֹחֵט, [read instead as though from the root meaning 'to slaughter']: for this one comes to slaughter others and is found himself slaughtered, for evil tongue kills three (Leviticus Rabbah 5:1). And likewise Korah was included in the category of the company of scoffers, as is found in the Midrash (Shochar Tov, Psalms 1), and a sign for the matter: 'Korah' is an acronym — קֶשֶׁת רוֹמַח חֶרֶב (bow, spear, sword), on account of what is written (Psalms 57:5): 'The sons of men, whose teeth are spears and arrows, and their tongue a sharp sword.' And the reason Korah was not punished with tzara'at like Moses is because he was held to a greater measure than that — for he denied the fundamental principle and the Torah of Moses, and sought to abolish the authority, and from this side justice determined that he should be among those swallowed up. Moreover, he had already seen the great deed of YHWH such as Israel had not seen in Moses' first mission. And this is what it says: 'A memorial to the children of Israel, etc., that no stranger who is not of the seed of Aaron may draw near to burn incense' (Numbers 17:5) — for the stranger who draws near presumably disputes the prophecy of Moses; and in any case the stranger who draws near shall not be equal to Korah and his assembly in punishment, for Korah and his assembly had in them great sins besides this; rather, he shall be 'as YHWH spoke to him by the hand of Moses' — just as Moses was stricken in his hand with tzara'at, so shall every one who disputes the priesthood be stricken with tzara'at."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:04.962027667Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}